  "versions": [
    {
      "meaning": "The number 100 symbolizes completeness, God's blessing, and abundance in biblical tradition.",
      "description": "Abraham received his son Isaac when he was 100 years old, highlighting God's faithfulness and ability to fulfill His promises even when it seems humanly impossible (Gen 21:5). In Jesus' parable of the sower, good soil yields a hundredfold harvest, symbolizing maximum fruitfulness and blessing. In Jewish tradition, there is also a custom to recite 100 blessings (berachot) daily, expressing complete devotion and gratitude to God.",
      "reason": "[minor] The Description contains an interesting fact about the 100 daily blessings (berachot), which is a rabbinic tradition. This somewhat breaks with the main biblical text and fits better as a footnote.; [minor] A Bible reference for the parable of the sower (Matt 13:8 / Mark 4:8) is missing. Bible references should be included for verifiability.; [minor] The Meaning field is good, but could be clarified with \"fulfillment of promises,\" which is a very central aspect of the number 100 (Abraham's story).; [minor] The number 100 as 10×10 (amplification of perfection) is a relevant structural point that can be mentioned in a footnote.",
      "score": 7,
      "date": "2026-04-04"
    }
  ],
  "footnotes": [
    {
      "text": "In Jewish tradition, it is customary to pray 100 blessings (berachot) each day, expressing total devotion and thankfulness to God. This tradition is attributed to King David in the Talmud (Menachot 43b).",
      "source": "rabbinsk"
    },
    {
      "text": "Symbolically, 100 can be understood as 10 × 10—an intensification of the number 10, which itself represents completeness and divine order. The hundred thus becomes the perfection of perfection.",
      "source": "kabbalistisk"
    }
  ]
}
